<!-- 9 · FAQ -->
<section class="faq rv section-stack" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 class="faq-heading h2-single" id="faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-list section-grid">
        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>Is my product code still mine?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Yes. You own the product you build: the features, flows, user experience, and product logic. Fluxzero provides the backend foundation and runtime underneath it.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>Can I export my data?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Yes. Your product data should never feel trapped. Fluxzero is built around clear product data and will support exporting it when you need to move, analyze, or back it up.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>How is Fluxzero different from Supabase, Firebase, or Vercel?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Those platforms give you services and building blocks. Fluxzero gives you an integrated product backend: auth, data, permissions, testing, deployment, and runtime behavior already designed to work together. You build the product layer on top.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>How is Fluxzero different from Lovable, Bolt, or Replit?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Those tools help you build or generate an app. You can use that output as the starting point for a Fluxzero app, so you do not have to start over.</p>
                <p>Fluxzero adds the backend foundation that lets the app become real software: something you can run, test, launch, and keep improving with users.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>What kinds of products is Fluxzero good for?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Fluxzero is a good fit for products with users, data, permissions, workflows, and business logic: SaaS apps, marketplaces, portals, internal tools, workflow products, and AI-enabled software.</p>
                <p>There is no scale ceiling on what you can build or run. Fluxzero is designed for products that may start small and grow into sophisticated, high-volume systems.</p>
                <p>It is not meant for simple landing pages, content-only sites, throwaway demos, or products where you specifically want to design every layer of infrastructure yourself.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>Do I need to know backend development?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>No. You need to understand the product you want to build. Your coding agent can work on features, flows, and behavior while Fluxzero keeps the backend foundation out of your prompt loop.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>Can developers take it further later?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Yes. Fluxzero gives your team a clean Java codebase focused on features, flows, and behavior, with behavior tests around the things that matter. Real developers can keep building in their own IDE, review the code, add more tests, and go deeper into the technical model when needed.</p>
                <p>That makes it easy to hand over when the product grows. Java talent is widely available, and because backend complexity stays out of the product layer, founders can keep building with AI alongside the team.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>Can I use Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, or my own IDE?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>Yes. Generate your app, unzip it, and open it in your coding agent or IDE. Fluxzero is designed to fit the way builders are already using AI to ship software.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
                <span>When do I pay?</span>
                <span class="faq-arrow">+</span>
            </button>
            <div class="faq-a">
                <p>You can start building before paying. You only pay when you go live.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="faq-help">Still wondering? <a href="/contact">Get in touch</a> and we'll help.</p>
</section>

